WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:15.975
As you go through the
examples that follow,

2
00:00:15.975 --> 00:00:18.060
you may notice that
some strategies

3
00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.100
use different mixes of products,

4
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.085
yet have similar risk
reward structures.

5
00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:26.040
This is because the
prices of puts and

6
00:00:26.040 --> 00:00:28.470
calls are inextricably
linked to each

7
00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:31.200
other and the price of the
underlying stock through

8
00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.750
an equation known
as put-call parity.

9
00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.810
The call price plus the
strike price is equal to

10
00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:43.130
the forward value of a stock
price plus the put price.

11
00:00:43.130 --> 00:00:46.625
It is important to use the
forward value of the stock,

12
00:00:46.625 --> 00:00:49.295
which is adjusted for
interest rates and dividends,

13
00:00:49.295 --> 00:00:52.865
rather than strictly the
current price of the stock.

14
00:00:52.865 --> 00:00:56.455
We can calculate the
forward value this way.

15
00:00:56.455 --> 00:00:58.520
The forward value equals

16
00:00:58.520 --> 00:01:00.350
the current value multiplied

17
00:01:00.350 --> 00:01:02.315
by one plus the interest rate

18
00:01:02.315 --> 00:01:04.910
times the days until
expiration divided

19
00:01:04.910 --> 00:01:09.020
by 365, less dividends.

20
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.180
In a low rates environment,

21
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:13.520
the forward value of
a stock that pays

22
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:17.210
no dividends is roughly
equal to the current value.

23
00:01:17.210 --> 00:01:20.210
If interest rates are high
or a stock is hard to

24
00:01:20.210 --> 00:01:21.890
borrow or the stock pays

25
00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.185
a dividend during the
life of the option,

26
00:01:24.185 --> 00:01:25.700
the forward value may differ

27
00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.615
meaningfully from the
current stock price.

28
00:01:28.615 --> 00:01:31.925
When we examined the
put-call parity formula,

29
00:01:31.925 --> 00:01:34.550
simple algebra shows
us to demonstrate how

30
00:01:34.550 --> 00:01:37.990
different structures
have similar payoffs.

31
00:01:37.990 --> 00:01:41.150
For example, the call
price is equal to

32
00:01:41.150 --> 00:01:42.530
the forward value minus

33
00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:45.755
the strike price
plus the put price,

34
00:01:45.755 --> 00:01:48.590
that shows that the value
of a call is the same as

35
00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.790
being longer stock
and longer put.

36
00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:53.585
You will notice that

37
00:01:53.585 --> 00:01:56.855
those path graphs
look quite similar.

38
00:01:56.855 --> 00:01:58.970
As you go through
the study guide,

39
00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.770
keep this equation
in mind when you

40
00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.355
see other similar looking graphs.

41
00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:05.945
If we structure the
equation this way,

42
00:02:05.945 --> 00:02:08.720
we see that a long call
and short put with

43
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:10.445
the same strike and exploration

44
00:02:10.445 --> 00:02:13.040
creates a synthetic future.

45
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:15.950
The forward value equals
the strike price plus

46
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:19.025
the call price minus
the put price.

47
00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:21.590
The difference between
the forward value

48
00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:22.610
and the current value

49
00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:26.320
of a stock is a function of
interest rates and dividends.

50
00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:28.890
Because option
prices are based on

51
00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:31.295
the forward value of
the underlying product,

52
00:02:31.295 --> 00:02:32.900
it is crucial that options

53
00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:35.150
investors consider the effects of

54
00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:37.295
dividends and interest rates

55
00:02:37.295 --> 00:02:40.090
when implementing
their strategies.

56
00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:43.800
Keep this in mind
as we move forward.